"The workload w.sshd has no sole\u2011owner elements, so the only reason the attack surface remains open is its use of the shared element sc.keyctl. sshd invokes the keyctl syscall to manage kernel keyrings for storing authentication tokens and session keys.  \n\nThrough sc.keyctl an attacker can trigger a use\u2011after\u2011free on keyring objects and a reference\u2011count overflow, giving the primitives of arbitrary kernel memory read/write and privilege escalation via corrupted keyring references.  \n\nIf the operator does not need keyring\u2011based authentication, they can disable the feature in sshd (e.g., turn off GSSAPIKeyExchange or apply a seccomp filter that blocks keyctl) which removes the surface; otherwise the surface must be retained because sshd\u2019s workflow depends on keyctl for its keyring operations."